{"markdown_body": "1. The launch fails to achieve team-wide adoption because the steep learning curve of keyboard-first navigation alienates non-power-user engineers and cross-functional collaborators who rely on visual, mouse-driven workflows, preventing full displacement of Jira. The early signal will be a sharp drop-off in daily active usage among non-core developers within the first two weeks of onboarding, with command-palette shortcut activation concentrated in under 20% of the team.

2. The launch fails because edge cases and webhook latency in the bi-directional GitHub integration corrupt automated cycle-time metrics, destroying engineering trust and forcing teams back to manual task management. The early signal will be a high frequency of manual status overrides and support tickets reporting desynced pull requests within the first two sprint cycles of repository integration.

3. The launch fails commercially because VPs of Engineering face executive and finance pushback against approving a $25 to $35 per-seat monthly premium for an engineering-only tool while the rest of the company remains anchored to legacy Jira licenses. The early signal will be successful single-pod technical evaluations that consistently stall at the procurement phase without expanding into department-wide paid seat conversions.", "node_id": "plan"}
